WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.660
In this presentation, we will introduce you to data

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.520
standards as they relate to biodiversity data.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.160
In particular, we will focus on the Darwin Core Standard.

4
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.620
The engineer and industrialist,

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.120
W. Edwards Deming said,

6
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.860
"Standardization does not mean that we all wear the same

7
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.820
color and weave of cloth, eat standard sandwiches,

8
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.240
or live in standard rooms with standard furniture.

9
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.260
Homes of infinite variety of design are

10
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.300
built with a few types of bricks and with lumber

11
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.250
of standard sizes and with water and heating pipes and

12
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.500
fittings of standard dimensions."

13
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.880
What he was trying to say is that using standardization does

14
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.300
not prevent us from being creative.

15
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.520
He was also showing us that we already live with standards

16
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.300
all around us.

17
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.160
As we move forward, we will define the term standard,

18
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.440
and we'll look at how we interact with standards every day.

19
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.140
We will then introduce you to biodiversity information standards,

20
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.600
including the Darwin Core Standard, which you will continue to use

21
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:24.760
throughout this course.

22
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.520
So, what is a standard?

23
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.100
At its simplest, it is an agreed way of

24
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:38.960
doing something.

25
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.740
Standards are combinations of norms,

26
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.460
conventions, specifications, requirements,

27
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.520
restrictions, and rules.

28
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.740
The main purpose of standards is to create a

29
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.230
framework of mutual understanding.

30
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.960
They should provide clarity and help communication.

31
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.420
Examples of everyday things that we encounter

32
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.980
which make use of standards to aid communication of

33
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.810
information are units of measurement,

34
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.400
numerical systems, alphabets, languages,

35
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:24.820
emojis,

36
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.240
postal addressing, Morse code, and bar

37
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:29.740
coding.

38
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.580
Let's take a very specific example and break it down.

39
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.040
Here, in order to communicate accurately and consistently a

40
00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.400
position on the Earth,

41
00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.540
latitude and longitude is actually a combination of at

42
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.580
least eight standards.

43
00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.660
Measurement is geographic coordinates, format is

44
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.700
degrees, minutes, and seconds, numerical system is

45
00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:06.020
hexadecimal, which is with a base of 60,

46
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:07.920
numbers

47
00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.640
are Indo-Arabic, language is English, alphabet is Latin,

48
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.840
symbols is typography, and the font is

49
00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:17.480
Roboto.

50
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.100
Standards provide ways of constraining the array of

51
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:30.960
possibilities.

52
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.600
In the earlier foundations presentation, you learned about types of

53
00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:39.080
data, schemas, formats, and character encodings.

54
00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.020
Each of these can be used to constrain the array of

55
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.340
possibilities within the terms of a standard.

56
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.260
Types of data

57
00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.190
can restrict the values of a field,

58
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.180
like alphanumeric text in a text field, decimal

59
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.980
in a float, and yes or no in a Boolean field.

60
00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.820
An encoding schema

61
00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.290
can restrict the range of values in a field.

62
00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.260
For example, the list of possible latitude values have a range

63
00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.221
between negative 90 and positive 90.

64
00:04:17.541 --> 00:04:21.200
A format can restrict the representation of data in a field.

65
00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:25.650
For example, whether a date appears as year, month, day,

66
00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.159
or day, month, year, or month, day, year.

67
00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.920
And then character encoding provides the rules

68
00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.020
for interpreting bytes of data.

69
00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.600
For our purpose, we will use UTF-8

70
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:42.440
format.

71
00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.630
During this course, you will be learning how to share your

72
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:53.060
data.

73
00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.440
As such, you will encounter standards for transferring data.

74
00:04:58.780 --> 00:04:59.900
An application schema

75
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.460
allows for combination of data standards for a

76
00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:05.560
specific purpose.

77
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.120
For example, using Darwin Core terms with Darwin Core

78
00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:10.800
archives.

79
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.180
We will dwell more into both of these in just a few

80
00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:15.740
moments.

81
00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:17.080
Again,

82
00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.360
you'll make use of format, but this time the format

83
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:21.940
restricts

84
00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.040
data structures.

85
00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.740
The data may make use of a CSV, a XML, a JSON,

86
00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:29.880
or a RDF.

87
00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.800
And lastly, you will have a transfer protocol, which

88
00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:38.590
provides information on how and where to send the information.

89
00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:41.560
These might include HTTP,

90
00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.380
the Hypertext Transfer Protocol, or FTP, the File Transfer

91
00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.060
Protocol.

92
00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.720
In the domain of biodiversity informatics, there are many

93
00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.680
standards already available that can help you work with your

94
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:02.440
data. The USGS has a very specific

95
00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:06.532
definition for thisData standards are the rules

96
00:06:07.012 --> 00:06:10.152
by which data are described and recorded.

97
00:06:10.972 --> 00:06:14.832
In order to share, exchange, and understand data, we

98
00:06:14.892 --> 00:06:17.712
must standardize the format as well as the meaning.

99
00:06:19.332 --> 00:06:23.052
The result of using these standards, where appropriate, is

100
00:06:23.112 --> 00:06:26.692
that you will increase data integrity, accuracy, and

101
00:06:26.772 --> 00:06:30.692
consistency by clarifying ambiguous meanings

102
00:06:31.312 --> 00:06:33.522
and minimizing redundant data.

103
00:06:34.372 --> 00:06:37.392
The standards that you are likely to encounter

104
00:06:39.032 --> 00:06:42.572
or use on a regular basis may include

105
00:06:42.972 --> 00:06:45.152
Ecological Metadata Language standard,

106
00:06:45.992 --> 00:06:49.902
EML, Humboldt Ecological Inventory, Humboldt

107
00:06:49.952 --> 00:06:50.532
Extension,

108
00:06:51.372 --> 00:06:54.732
Global Genome Biodiversity Network, GGBN,

109
00:06:56.132 --> 00:06:59.452
Ocean Data Standards and Best Practices project,

110
00:07:00.032 --> 00:07:01.052
ODSBP.

111
00:07:02.212 --> 00:07:04.252
And the last but not least,

112
00:07:05.152 --> 00:07:06.012
we will spend

113
00:07:07.192 --> 00:07:10.332
the rest of this discussion on Darwin Core.

114
00:07:10.992 --> 00:07:14.932
The Darwin Core Standard will allow you to share your occurrence,

115
00:07:15.132 --> 00:07:17.432
taxonomic, and event data sets.

116
00:07:22.232 --> 00:07:26.212
Darwin Core is a biodiversity standard developed

117
00:07:26.292 --> 00:07:29.092
by the biodiversity informatics community.

118
00:07:29.752 --> 00:07:33.612
It was originally developed under the Taxonomic Databases Working

119
00:07:33.672 --> 00:07:37.492
Group, or TDWG, pronounced "tad-wig".

120
00:07:38.492 --> 00:07:42.172
In recent years, the group has been renamed to

121
00:07:42.212 --> 00:07:45.752
Biodiversity Information Standards, but the acronym

122
00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:46.412
persists

123
00:07:47.612 --> 00:07:51.012
as the community is quite fond of the name TDWG.

124
00:07:52.192 --> 00:07:54.872
The standard includes a glossary of terms.

125
00:07:55.952 --> 00:07:58.852
In other contexts, these might be called

126
00:07:59.772 --> 00:08:02.732
properties, elements, fields,

127
00:08:03.252 --> 00:08:05.772
columns, attributes, or concepts,

128
00:08:06.872 --> 00:08:10.862
intended to facilitate the sharing of information

129
00:08:10.892 --> 00:08:13.971
about biological diversity by providing

130
00:08:14.172 --> 00:08:17.092
identifiers, labels, and definitions.

131
00:08:18.092 --> 00:08:21.452
Darwin Core is primarily based on taxa,

132
00:08:22.352 --> 00:08:25.852
their occurrences in nature as documented by

133
00:08:25.932 --> 00:08:29.752
observations, specimens, samples, and related

134
00:08:29.852 --> 00:08:33.732
information. So, in short, Darwin Core

135
00:08:33.812 --> 00:08:37.472
is a list of fields and their definitions

136
00:08:37.632 --> 00:08:40.312
as they relate to biodiversity data.

137
00:08:46.052 --> 00:08:49.992
As we dive deeper into Darwin Core, or as it is

138
00:08:50.032 --> 00:08:51.592
abbreviated, DWC,

139
00:08:52.492 --> 00:08:55.612
you will learn it is more than just a list of fields.

140
00:08:56.892 --> 00:08:59.972
We will use Simple Darwin Core, which is a

141
00:09:00.312 --> 00:09:04.092
predefined subset of terms that have common use

142
00:09:04.172 --> 00:09:07.792
across a wide variety of biodiversity applications.

143
00:09:08.772 --> 00:09:12.632
This subset contains more than 150 fields that

144
00:09:12.692 --> 00:09:16.512
are placed into a set of field classes comprised

145
00:09:16.612 --> 00:09:16.792
of

146
00:09:17.632 --> 00:09:20.452
Record &amp; Dataset, Occurrence,

147
00:09:21.052 --> 00:09:24.952
Organism, Material Entity, Material Sample,

148
00:09:25.592 --> 00:09:29.232
Event, Location, Geological Context,

149
00:09:30.752 --> 00:09:31.632
Identification,

150
00:09:32.632 --> 00:09:36.321
and Taxon. Additionally, there are two auxiliary

151
00:09:36.392 --> 00:09:40.172
classes called Resource Relationship and

152
00:09:40.232 --> 00:09:41.712
Measurement of Artefact.

153
00:09:43.592 --> 00:09:45.972
From the Simple Darwin Core User Guide:

154
00:09:47.052 --> 00:09:50.192
Simple Darwin Core is simple in that it

155
00:09:50.252 --> 00:09:51.792
assumes and allows

156
00:09:52.672 --> 00:09:56.402
no structure beyond the concept of rows and columns,

157
00:09:57.032 --> 00:10:00.992
which might be thought of as attributes and their values or

158
00:10:01.032 --> 00:10:02.512
fields and records.

159
00:10:08.412 --> 00:10:12.032
The Darwin Core Quick Reference Guide will soon become

160
00:10:12.312 --> 00:10:16.072
your go-to resource. This page provides a list of

161
00:10:16.512 --> 00:10:20.032
all currently recommended terms of the Darwin Core standard.

162
00:10:20.972 --> 00:10:24.872
Categories such as Occurrence or Event correspond to the

163
00:10:24.891 --> 00:10:28.252
Darwin Core classes, which group other terms.

164
00:10:34.072 --> 00:10:37.932
We now will look at some examples of Darwin Core

165
00:10:37.972 --> 00:10:41.832
terms. The quick reference guide consistently

166
00:10:41.872 --> 00:10:44.892
displays each term with the identifier name,

167
00:10:45.352 --> 00:10:45.972
definition,

168
00:10:46.852 --> 00:10:48.512
comments, and examples.

169
00:10:49.472 --> 00:10:53.312
The first terms we will review are country and country

170
00:10:53.322 --> 00:10:55.892
code within the location category.

171
00:10:56.652 --> 00:10:59.472
Generally, data holders have a field for

172
00:10:59.532 --> 00:11:03.172
country within their source data, but often this

173
00:11:03.192 --> 00:11:06.492
data can be very messy with misspellings,

174
00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:09.452
abbreviations, and historical names.

175
00:11:10.112 --> 00:11:14.092
However, it is one of the most easily standardized pieces

176
00:11:14.152 --> 00:11:14.712
of data.

177
00:11:15.532 --> 00:11:19.472
As noted in the comments, the recommended best

178
00:11:19.592 --> 00:11:23.332
practice is to use a controlled vocabulary

179
00:11:23.352 --> 00:11:26.632
such as the Getty Thesaurus of Geographic

180
00:11:26.712 --> 00:11:29.572
Names. A controlled vocabulary places

181
00:11:29.612 --> 00:11:31.592
restrictions on the values

182
00:11:33.052 --> 00:11:34.912
that should be used in that term.

183
00:11:36.592 --> 00:11:40.412
Country code is a term that is generally not present in holder

184
00:11:40.512 --> 00:11:44.242
data, but again, it is another field that

185
00:11:44.512 --> 00:11:48.492
can easily be supplied with data due to recommendation in

186
00:11:48.552 --> 00:11:51.292
the comments to use an ISO

187
00:11:51.472 --> 00:11:55.292
3166-1 Alpha-2 country code.

188
00:11:56.372 --> 00:11:59.912
GBIF strongly recommends the sharing of country code

189
00:11:59.992 --> 00:12:03.692
within occurrence datasets. Sharing of country is also

190
00:12:03.772 --> 00:12:06.732
encouraged. You will learn more about the GBIF

191
00:12:06.932 --> 00:12:10.352
requirements and recommendations in later

192
00:12:10.432 --> 00:12:16.962
sessionsThe

193
00:12:17.042 --> 00:12:19.442
next term is basisOfRecord.

194
00:12:20.102 --> 00:12:23.922
BasisOfRecord defines the nature of each record in a

195
00:12:23.942 --> 00:12:24.482
dataset.

196
00:12:25.682 --> 00:12:29.282
BasisOfRecord follows a controlled vocabulary.

197
00:12:29.942 --> 00:12:31.162
You can choose from

198
00:12:32.162 --> 00:12:36.082
preservedSpecimen, fossilSpecimen, livingSpecimen,

199
00:12:36.622 --> 00:12:40.282
materialSample, event, humanObservation,

200
00:12:40.862 --> 00:12:44.202
machineObservation, taxon, or occurrence.

201
00:12:45.002 --> 00:12:48.362
GBIF requires basisOfRecord published

202
00:12:48.602 --> 00:12:50.762
with occurrence datasets.

203
00:12:55.722 --> 00:12:59.122
The last term we will review is occurrenceID.

204
00:12:59.942 --> 00:13:03.482
When publishing occurrence records, GBIF requires an

205
00:13:03.722 --> 00:13:04.522
occurrence ID.

206
00:13:05.402 --> 00:13:09.122
An occurrence ID is an identifier for the occurrence

207
00:13:09.182 --> 00:13:12.562
itself, not the digital record of the occurrence.

208
00:13:13.562 --> 00:13:17.262
Recommended best practice is to use a globally unique

209
00:13:17.322 --> 00:13:21.102
identifier, otherwise known as a GUID.

210
00:13:22.382 --> 00:13:25.802
In the absence of a GUID, a unique identifier

211
00:13:26.342 --> 00:13:30.222
can be comprised of other identifiers

212
00:13:30.282 --> 00:13:31.382
within the dataset.

213
00:13:32.682 --> 00:13:36.642
There are tools on the internet that can help you to generate

214
00:13:36.782 --> 00:13:40.482
GUIDs for your records. If you use this

215
00:13:40.582 --> 00:13:44.482
method, these GUIDs should become a

216
00:13:44.602 --> 00:13:48.442
permanent field within your source data

217
00:13:48.782 --> 00:13:50.402
identifying each record.

218
00:13:51.802 --> 00:13:55.582
For the practice done in this course, you will create

219
00:13:55.802 --> 00:13:59.782
an occurrence ID with a format similar to the third

220
00:13:59.882 --> 00:14:02.302
example here in the blue box.

221
00:14:07.302 --> 00:14:09.022
In using simple Darwin Core,

222
00:14:09.882 --> 00:14:13.222
you may discover that you have more data to share,

223
00:14:13.922 --> 00:14:17.322
but you cannot find corresponding terms in

224
00:14:18.042 --> 00:14:18.802
DWC.

225
00:14:20.082 --> 00:14:23.802
This data could be image or sound

226
00:14:23.902 --> 00:14:24.302
file.

227
00:14:25.682 --> 00:14:29.662
Or perhaps you are responsible for a collection of vertebrates

228
00:14:29.702 --> 00:14:33.391
and you have extensive data compiled on the weights and

229
00:14:33.922 --> 00:14:37.502
size of the specimens, or even detailed historical

230
00:14:37.562 --> 00:14:40.422
information on the identification of taxon.

231
00:14:41.402 --> 00:14:45.122
When this occurs, you will look to Darwin Core extensions

232
00:14:46.062 --> 00:14:49.922
so that you can extend the base data

233
00:14:49.982 --> 00:14:53.862
by providing additional files that correspond to

234
00:14:53.902 --> 00:14:54.862
the base data.

235
00:14:55.842 --> 00:14:59.602
Extensions that would meet your needs in

236
00:14:59.622 --> 00:15:02.982
these three examples are simple

237
00:15:03.062 --> 00:15:05.962
multimedia, measurements or facts,

238
00:15:07.242 --> 00:15:08.722
and identification history.

239
00:15:09.622 --> 00:15:13.342
There are many more extensions. GBIF maintains a list of

240
00:15:13.462 --> 00:15:16.922
all approved and draft extensions on its

241
00:15:16.962 --> 00:15:18.062
tools subsite.

242
00:15:22.842 --> 00:15:26.282
There are many layers in our biodiversity informatics

243
00:15:26.362 --> 00:15:26.842
community.

244
00:15:27.902 --> 00:15:31.882
The image here shows the relationships between these

245
00:15:31.982 --> 00:15:35.622
layers and where they intersect with the

246
00:15:35.642 --> 00:15:39.502
Darwin Core and where extensions may be necessary

247
00:15:40.082 --> 00:15:42.402
in order to fully share the data.

248
00:15:48.422 --> 00:15:51.422
Data shared to GBIF is currently

249
00:15:51.502 --> 00:15:54.842
submitted via a Darwin Core Archive or

250
00:15:54.962 --> 00:15:56.662
DWC-A.

251
00:15:57.992 --> 00:16:01.582
A DWC-A is an expression of the

252
00:16:01.602 --> 00:16:03.182
Darwin Core text guide.

253
00:16:04.442 --> 00:16:05.122
It is a

254
00:16:06.002 --> 00:16:09.662
compressed file containing a minimum of three

255
00:16:09.742 --> 00:16:10.222
files.

256
00:16:11.262 --> 00:16:13.802
It is encoded as UTF-8.

257
00:16:15.122 --> 00:16:16.382
In this example,

258
00:16:17.502 --> 00:16:17.862
these

259
00:16:18.882 --> 00:16:21.722
three files are a data file,

260
00:16:21.902 --> 00:16:24.002
occurrence.txt,

261
00:16:25.262 --> 00:16:29.062
conforming to the simple DWC in a

262
00:16:29.122 --> 00:16:32.882
CSV format, where the first row includes

263
00:16:32.902 --> 00:16:35.192
Darwin Core standard term names.

264
00:16:37.082 --> 00:16:39.202
A meta file, meta.xml,

265
00:16:40.102 --> 00:16:43.142
in XML format

266
00:16:43.622 --> 00:16:47.202
containing technical details to instruct a

267
00:16:47.242 --> 00:16:51.102
computer in how to use the data file.

268
00:16:52.082 --> 00:16:56.002
A meta file, EML.xml,

269
00:16:57.102 --> 00:16:59.462
is XML format containing

270
00:17:01.562 --> 00:17:04.922
explanatory details about the records

271
00:17:05.242 --> 00:17:09.162
contained within the data file to instruct a user

272
00:17:09.623 --> 00:17:10.302
if the data

273
00:17:11.182 --> 00:17:13.222
will be fit for their use.

274
00:17:14.222 --> 00:17:18.162
A more complex structure can be obtained by sharing

275
00:17:18.222 --> 00:17:21.932
multiple related CSV files to extend the

276
00:17:22.002 --> 00:17:23.742
data. They are related

277
00:17:24.562 --> 00:17:27.982
to the core file by the way of a unique ID.

278
00:17:29.822 --> 00:17:33.302
In an occurrence dataset, these related

279
00:17:33.362 --> 00:17:37.062
CSV files are related by the occurrence

280
00:17:37.142 --> 00:17:37.402
ID.

281
00:17:42.842 --> 00:17:44.562
So while Darwin Core Archives

282
00:17:45.422 --> 00:17:48.902
has been our preferred method of publishing since

283
00:17:49.042 --> 00:17:49.652
2012,

284
00:17:51.522 --> 00:17:54.182
we are approaching a new model

285
00:17:55.002 --> 00:17:57.382
called Darwin Core Data Package.

286
00:17:58.262 --> 00:18:01.862
The new model is intended to broaden the range

287
00:18:02.042 --> 00:18:05.862
of scientific questions that GBIF data can address.

288
00:18:07.142 --> 00:18:10.062
It will allow us to extend our data scope,

289
00:18:10.902 --> 00:18:14.862
engage with new data communities, and build tools

290
00:18:14.922 --> 00:18:17.342
to enable data flows based on

291
00:18:18.402 --> 00:18:18.942
the updated

292
00:18:19.062 --> 00:18:24.552
standardThe

293
00:18:24.592 --> 00:18:28.432
community has been developing this model since

294
00:18:28.592 --> 00:18:32.572
2022, and the standard developments for this model has

295
00:18:32.632 --> 00:18:35.972
been in development since 2023.

296
00:18:37.092 --> 00:18:40.942
We are finally in the community review and ratification

297
00:18:41.092 --> 00:18:44.992
phase for the new terms and the new data

298
00:18:45.072 --> 00:18:48.692
package, which was released in October

299
00:18:48.792 --> 00:18:50.332
of 2025.

300
00:18:51.152 --> 00:18:55.052
As you can see from this timeline, it takes a long time

301
00:18:55.062 --> 00:18:58.781
to implement change to an established standard,

302
00:18:59.012 --> 00:19:02.392
and it takes a dedicated community to see it through.

303
00:19:09.022 --> 00:19:12.942
The changes under public review include 65

304
00:19:13.072 --> 00:19:16.992
new terms, 75 proposed changes to

305
00:19:17.052 --> 00:19:20.812
existing terms, a documented conceptual model

306
00:19:21.172 --> 00:19:24.872
for DWC, and guidelines for using the

307
00:19:24.932 --> 00:19:26.652
frictionless data format.

308
00:19:31.752 --> 00:19:35.212
The Darwin Core Data Package completely opens up

309
00:19:35.512 --> 00:19:39.352
how much data can be shared, taking a Darwin

310
00:19:39.432 --> 00:19:43.212
Core Archive short story to a Darwin Core

311
00:19:43.252 --> 00:19:45.012
Data Package epic novel.

312
00:19:45.992 --> 00:19:49.372
For comparison, stories can be told with either

313
00:19:49.452 --> 00:19:52.792
DWCA or DWCDP,

314
00:19:53.512 --> 00:19:57.392
but it should be evident that the range of scientific endeavor

315
00:19:58.032 --> 00:20:00.932
that can be faithfully captured by

316
00:20:00.992 --> 00:20:04.192
DWCDP is much more vast.

317
00:20:10.652 --> 00:20:12.532
All of this might be overwhelming

318
00:20:13.332 --> 00:20:17.272
when you are just learning about data mobilization, but we want you

319
00:20:17.372 --> 00:20:20.772
to know that changes are coming, which,

320
00:20:20.812 --> 00:20:24.612
depending on your data, might be very exciting for you.

321
00:20:25.952 --> 00:20:29.372
We also recognize that not everyone needs to do

322
00:20:30.152 --> 00:20:32.132
all of that, especially

323
00:20:33.412 --> 00:20:37.172
all within one dataset, and publishers will be able to use

324
00:20:37.572 --> 00:20:38.532
only what they need.

325
00:20:39.592 --> 00:20:43.432
For the time being, with regards to this course, we are

326
00:20:43.492 --> 00:20:44.352
focusing on

327
00:20:45.412 --> 00:20:49.112
training using the Event Core, which you will

328
00:20:49.212 --> 00:20:52.632
learn more about in the coming sessions.

329
00:20:53.752 --> 00:20:57.732
When the ratification process has completed and we are

330
00:20:57.772 --> 00:21:01.732
ready for publishers to begin using the Darwin Core Data

331
00:21:01.812 --> 00:21:05.252
Package, we will organize virtual community events

332
00:21:05.912 --> 00:21:07.472
to supplement training.

333
00:21:13.772 --> 00:21:15.032
So as we conclude,

334
00:21:15.972 --> 00:21:19.792
we have covered what Darwin Core is and hopefully you

335
00:21:19.812 --> 00:21:23.692
have started to develop a sense of why you should use

336
00:21:23.752 --> 00:21:23.952
it.

337
00:21:24.952 --> 00:21:27.712
It is a standard and standards are good.

338
00:21:28.772 --> 00:21:32.032
Standards provide us with the rules and

339
00:21:32.132 --> 00:21:35.592
protocols we need to share our data with others.

340
00:21:37.112 --> 00:21:40.612
Darwin Core also provides us with a common language.

341
00:21:41.652 --> 00:21:45.192
As we saw in the foundations documentation presentation,

342
00:21:46.592 --> 00:21:50.332
source data can be tricky when trying to

343
00:21:50.372 --> 00:21:51.532
compare datasets.

344
00:21:52.732 --> 00:21:56.612
The fields in your source data might be different

345
00:21:57.212 --> 00:22:00.372
than the fields in another institution's data source.

346
00:22:01.832 --> 00:22:05.532
When we all use Darwin Core to share our data, we

347
00:22:05.572 --> 00:22:09.292
understand that data has been shared with a

348
00:22:09.332 --> 00:22:10.232
common language.

349
00:22:16.392 --> 00:22:18.632
And it's not only the data holders

350
00:22:19.452 --> 00:22:21.332
that understand this common language.

351
00:22:22.232 --> 00:22:24.172
The data users do as well.

352
00:22:25.252 --> 00:22:26.632
And after all,

353
00:22:27.732 --> 00:22:31.652
what could be better than a user finding a dataset

354
00:22:32.252 --> 00:22:33.992
that is fit for their use,

355
00:22:34.932 --> 00:22:38.692
shared in a common language that allows them to do better

356
00:22:38.792 --> 00:22:39.292
science?

